(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-pdm-exam-2-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 45-year-old has a C-reactive protein checked during an acute infection and again after recovery. How does the level behave?","choices":["It rises slowly and stays high for years","It rises quickly and drops rapidly","It stays constant throughout life","It falls during active infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. C-reactive protein is an acute-phase reactant from the liver: it rises quickly in response to inflammation and drops rapidly once the process resolves. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 60-year-old asks why VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) remnants and LDL (low-density lipoprotein) all count toward plaque. What do they share?","choices":["Each carries apoA-I and removes cholesterol from the wall","Each is an apoB particle small enough to enter the artery wall","Each is made in the intestine from dietary fat after meals","Each is too large to cross the endothelium into the wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each apoB (apolipoprotein B) particle under about 70 nm can cross the endothelium and become trapped in the artery wall, initiating plaque. That is why therapy aims to reduce the number of circulating apoB particles. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which esophageal conditions contraindicate a transesophageal echocardiogram?","choices":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease","A history of esophageal candidiasis","Stricture, malignancy or bleeding varices","Recent barium swallow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three mean passing a probe risks perforation or hemorrhage, which is the hazard the contraindication list exists to avoid. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why is a transesophageal study used in a patient presenting with cerebral ischemia?","choices":["It measures cerebral blood flow directly and noninvasively","It assesses the carotid arteries","It can find thrombus in the left atrial appendage","It excludes coronary stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It looks for the source rather than the consequence, and the appendage is the site the transthoracic study sees worst. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Over which part of the action potential does the ABSOLUTE refractory period run?","choices":["From the middle of phase 3 to the end of phase 3","Throughout phase 4","From phase 0 to the middle of phase 3","From phase 1 to phase 2 only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it lasts about a hundred and eighty milliseconds, during which no stimulus of any strength will produce another action potential. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 56-year-old has just started a statin. When should the lipid panel be rechecked?","choices":["In 4 to 12 weeks","In 5 years","In 1 week","Only if he has symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Check lipids 4 to 12 weeks after starting or changing the dose of lipid-lowering therapy, then every 6 to 12 months. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man's chart lists a high-sensitivity troponin. What does the term high-sensitivity describe?","choices":["A fourth troponin subunit","A skeletal muscle troponin","The assay's characteristics","A troponin corrected for age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is not a different subunit of troponin. The assay detects circulating troponin at lower concentrations, which is where its advantage comes from. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"No calipers are available and a rhythm may be irregular. What is the most appropriate way to compare consecutive R to R intervals?","choices":["A count of complexes in three seconds","The three hundred method on one interval","A marked sheet of paper","The QT interval between two beats"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Marking two R waves on a paper edge and sliding it along the strip does the job of calipers for comparing consecutive R to R intervals. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What does the P wave represent, and how should it appear in lead II?","choices":["Atrial repolarization, appearing as a negative deflection","Ventricular depolarization, appearing as a positive deflection","Atrial depolarization, appearing as a positive deflection","The resting state between beats"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with a normal duration under three small boxes, and it is the first upward deflection before the QRS complex. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What advantages does cardiac computed tomography have over echocardiography?","choices":["Superior resolution and noninvasive coronary assessment","Assessment of cardiac function as well as anatomy","Freedom from ionizing radiation","Independence from heart rate and rhythm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with fast acquisition and interpretation as well, which together make it the anatomical study of choice. It also shows internal structures directly, and acquires and reports quickly. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 55-year-old asks how LDL (low-density lipoprotein) forms in the circulation. What is the sequence?","choices":["HDL gains triglyceride, becoming a remnant, then LDL","VLDL loses triglyceride, becoming a remnant, then LDL","Chylomicrons gain cholesterol in the liver, then LDL","LDL loses cholesterol, becoming a remnant, then VLDL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The liver secretes triglyceride-rich VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein); lipoprotein lipase strips triglyceride to give a remnant (IDL, intermediate-density lipoprotein) and then cholesterol-rich LDL. It is the same apolipoprotein B particle at different stages. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has thrombocytopenia, and his smear shows inclusions inside neutrophils. Which disorder fits?","choices":["May-Hegglin anomaly","Gray platelet syndrome","Immune thrombocytopenia","Hemophilia B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophil inclusions on the smear point to May-Hegglin anomaly, a myosin heavy chain 9 disorder, among the morphologic causes of platelet abnormality. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has calf cramping and a low suspicion of thrombosis. In which situation does a D-dimer add the most value?","choices":["When a normal result is expected","When a clot is already known to be present","When the clot location is needed","When platelet function is in doubt"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is most useful for its negative predictive value: a normal result helps exclude thrombosis, so it adds value only when a normal result is plausible. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 50-year-old with a body mass index of 44 has orthopnea and edema, yet a B-type natriuretic peptide below the heart failure cutoff. What explains the discrepancy?","choices":["Obesity falsely lowers natriuretic peptides","Obesity falsely raises natriuretic peptides","Obesity makes troponin falsely positive","Obesity makes the assay invalid entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A high body mass index is associated with falsely low natriuretic peptide levels, so a normal-looking value does not reassure in this patient. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What are contiguous leads?","choices":["Leads placed physically next to one another on the skin","Two or more leads looking at the same area of the heart","Leads that share a single physical electrode","Leads recorded at the same moment in time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why a change appearing in a contiguous group means more than the same change in one isolated lead. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man not on warfarin has an international normalized ratio of 2.6 (reference 0.8 to 1.2). Which cause fits?","choices":["Immune thrombocytopenia","Liver disease","Hemophilia A","Acute thrombosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Off warfarin, an abnormal prothrombin time or international normalized ratio may indicate a bleeding disorder, a clotting disorder, liver disease or vitamin K deficiency. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the Q wave represent, and why is it negative in lead II?","choices":["Depolarization of the anterior left ventricle, traveling toward lead II","Depolarization of the lateral wall of the left ventricle","Repolarization of the ventricles","Depolarization of the interventricular septum, which travels away from lead II"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The direction of that particular wavefront is what makes the deflection downward, and it is not always visible. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has had crushing chest pain with diaphoresis for 40 minutes. Which biomarker testing is first-line?","choices":["A single creatine kinase level","Serial myoglobin levels","Serial high-sensitivity troponin","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serial values are the point: a rising or falling pattern identifies acute injury, and the initial troponin also carries prognostic weight in acute coronary syndrome. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Reading left to right across the paper measures time. What does reading up and down measure?","choices":["Rate","Voltage","Time in a second dimension","Conduction velocity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and at standard calibration a small box is a tenth of a millivolt, with a ten millimeter deflection equal to one millivolt. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 57-year-old has an apoB (apolipoprotein B) level measured. Which particles carry apoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100)?","choices":["Chylomicrons and HDL only","HDL, VLDL and chylomicrons","VLDL, IDL, LDL and lipoprotein(a)","Lipoprotein(a) and HDL only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ApoB-100 is synthesized by the liver and found on very low-density, intermediate-density and low-density lipoprotein and on lipoprotein(a), one per particle. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"On what principle does radionuclide myocardial perfusion imaging work?","choices":["Diseased myocardium takes up more tracer than normal myocardium","Tracer is taken up only by scarred tissue","The tracer is metabolized into a contrast agent within the coronary arteries","Under stress, diseased myocardium receives less blood flow than normal myocardium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so comparing stress images against rest images is what makes the difference visible rather than either set alone. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has normal clotting screens and bleeding with skin bruising, petechiae and mucous membrane bleeding. Which category fits?","choices":["A platelet disorder","Factor VII deficiency","Hemophilia A","Hypofibrinogenemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Normal screens with skin bruising, petechiae and mucosal bleeding suggest platelet disorders or mild von Willebrand disease, evaluated with platelet function and von Willebrand studies. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which of these is a provider-determined reason to end an exercise stress test?","choices":["Exertional hypotension","The patient wanting to stop","Leg cramps or joint discomfort","A workload target set in advance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fall in systolic pressure below the resting standing value is a provider-determined endpoint, alongside extreme pressures, a patient who looks unwell and dangerous rhythms. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why is cardiac computed tomography synchronized with an electrocardiogram?","choices":["To slow the heart during the scan","To view different stages of the cardiac cycle","To provoke ischemia while images are taken","To locate the origin of an arrhythmia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because the heart is constantly moving, tying the scan to the tracing lets the heart be evaluated at chosen points in the cycle. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman takes warfarin, and her international normalized ratio is 4.8 (goal 2.0 to 3.0). What is the concern?","choices":["Clot risk","Bleeding risk","Platelet excess","Fibrinolysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. On warfarin a high international normalized ratio means over-anticoagulation and a risk of bleeding. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has an isolated prolonged partial thromboplastin time that does not correct when mixed one to one with normal plasma. What does this suggest?","choices":["Factor VIII deficiency","Factor IX deficiency","An inhibitor","Vitamin K deficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When a mixing study does not correct, an inhibitor is present, such as a specific factor inhibitor or lupus anticoagulant. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with severe malabsorption has a prolonged prothrombin time. Which liver-made protein, dependent on vitamin K, explains this?","choices":["Fibrin","Prothrombin","Plasmin","von Willebrand factor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prothrombin is produced by the liver and depends on vitamin K intake and absorption, so vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 61-year-old has a low HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol), below her lab's reference limit. How is that value used?","choices":["As the main target of therapy","As proof of familial disease","As a value to ignore","As a marker, not a target"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Higher HDL-C is generally favorable, but low HDL-C is a marker, not a therapeutic goal; treatment is aimed at the apoB (apolipoprotein B) particles. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A laboratory runs a prothrombin time on a 49-year-old woman. What is added to her plasma to start the clot?","choices":["A phospholipid activator","Plasmin","Tissue factor","Collagen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The prothrombin time is the time for a fibrin clot to form in a lab tube when tissue factor is added to the patient's plasma. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does cardiovascular magnetic resonance avoid that computed tomography does not?","choices":["Both ionizing radiation and iodinated contrast","Dependence on the operator","Any need for a resting heart rate","Sedation and airway monitoring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both avoided at once, which matters most in the young patient and in the patient with impaired kidneys. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a prolonged partial thromboplastin time and a normal platelet count. Which phase of hemostasis is most likely affected?","choices":["Primary hemostasis","Fibrinolysis","Vasoconstriction","Secondary hemostasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The partial thromboplastin time tests the intrinsic and common coagulation pathways, so a prolonged result points to the clotting factors, secondary hemostasis. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What characterizes phase 1, early repolarization?","choices":["Calcium channels open, producing contraction","Sodium channels close and potassium channels reopen","Fast sodium channels open","The membrane returns fully to minus ninety millivolts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The inward current stops and an outward one resumes, which begins bringing the membrane back down. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 58-year-old is evaluated for exertional chest pain rated 10 out of 10. Which question are cardiac biomarkers ordered to answer?","choices":["What is the long-term atherosclerotic risk?","How severe is any coronary stenosis?","Which coronary artery is occluded?","Is there acute cardiac injury or stress?"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Biomarkers are the acute question. The lipid panel answers a different one, the patient's long-term atherosclerotic risk, which is why it belongs to the routine physical rather than the chest-pain workup. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Why is the corrected QT used rather than the raw QT interval?","choices":["The QT interval changes with heart rate","The raw QT cannot be measured accurately on standard paper","The corrected value uses different units","The raw QT applies only to men"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Without correcting, the same heart would appear to have a different QT at different rates. The correction accounts for it. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph findings are given as indicators of cardiac pathology?","choices":["Coronary artery stenosis and the distribution of plaque","Edema, effusion, cardiomegaly, cephalization and calcification","Ejection fraction and regional wall motion abnormality","Valve pressure gradients and regurgitant volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with tension physiology completing the list. Several are about the lungs rather than the heart, which is the point: the heart announces itself through what it does to everything around it. The calcification may be of the great vessels, the valves or the pericardium, and tension physiology completes the list. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In what order do the five phases of the cardiac action potential occur?","choices":["Phase 0, then 1, 2, 3 and 4","Phase 1, then 2, 3, 4 and 0","Phase 4, then 3, 2, 1 and 0","Phase 4, then 0, 1, 2 and 3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The numbering does not run in order, which is exactly why it is worth learning as a sequence rather than as a list: rest is phase four, and the upstroke that follows it is phase zero. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 38-year-old has a markedly elevated C-reactive protein, far above the healthy range of less than 0.3 mg/dL. Which process typically produces the highest levels?","choices":["Acute bacterial infection","Uncomplicated obesity","Cigarette smoking","Normal pregnancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Overall, the highest levels occur with bacterial infections; severe elevation above 50 mg/dL is generally seen in acute bacterial infection. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 48-year-old has triglycerides of 900 mg/dL (desirable below 150 mg/dL). Which acute risk does this level carry?","choices":["Pancreatitis","Rhabdomyolysis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides of 500 mg/dL or above are severe hypertriglyceridemia with pancreatitis risk, and 1000 mg/dL or above is extreme. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A heart is being paced at a rate in the forties, with no sinoatrial activity. Which pacemaker is most likely responsible?","choices":["The sinoatrial node","The Purkinje network","The atrioventricular node","The bundle of His alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Its intrinsic rate is forty to sixty, and it takes over as the backup when the sinoatrial node fails. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How is the ATRIAL rate obtained by the six-second method?","choices":["Count the QRS complexes and multiply by ten","Count the P waves in six seconds and multiply by ten","Count the T waves and multiply by ten","Measure the PR interval and divide into sixty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and doing both counts separately is what reveals a heart block, where the two rates differ. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does a BIPOLAR lead measure?","choices":["The voltage difference between two distinct body points","The electrical activity from one electrode reference point","The average of all ten electrodes simultaneously","The mechanical movement of the chamber beneath it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two real electrodes with a defined polarity, which is what separates leads I, II and III from the rest. One positive and one negative. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which study is used to evaluate a patient with claudication and suspected peripheral arterial disease?","choices":["Transthoracic echocardiography","Non-contrast cardiac computed tomography","Arterial vascular ultrasound","Electrophysiological testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Evaluation of claudication and peripheral arterial disease is a listed indication, and it is fast, portable and free of ionizing radiation. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which complications of exercise stress testing are described?","choices":["Contrast-induced nephropathy","Malignancy induced by radiation exposure","Esophageal perforation","Arrhythmia, angina, infarction and death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The test deliberately provokes the thing it is looking for, which is why it is supervised and why it has a stopping rule. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has thrombocytopenia, and her smear shows large, pale gray platelets. Which syndrome fits?","choices":["May-Hegglin anomaly","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Spurious thrombocytopenia","Gray platelet syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrothrombocytopenia with gray platelets on the smear is gray platelet syndrome. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which is a listed indication for coronary angiography?","choices":["Screening an asymptomatic patient","Suspected deep venous thrombosis","Newly diagnosed atrial fibrillation alone","Severe angina despite medical therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, together with acute infarction, a high-risk noninvasive test result, high-risk patients undergoing non-cardiac surgery, and arrhythmias suspected to be vascular in origin. Or unstable angina. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What does echocardiography evaluate?","choices":["The coronary artery calcium score","The distribution of coronary atherosclerotic plaque","Chamber size, ventricular function, valves, shunts and pressures","The electrical conduction pathways of the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the functional study of the heart, which is what separates it from computed tomography, and Doppler is what adds flow and velocity to it. Wall thickness and ejection fraction come with the ventricular assessment, and Doppler adds flow and velocity. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with rest angina has serial troponins that stay below the 99th percentile limit. What do the results indicate?","choices":["A small infarction has occurred","The test is invalid in angina","Heart failure is excluded","No cardiac muscle injury has occurred"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Normal troponins in unstable angina mean no myocyte injury. Elevated troponins would mean injury, which stratifies risk and prompts consideration of revascularization. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 57-year-old's clinician chooses to follow non-HDL-C (non-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) rather than LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol). Why?","choices":["It is measured directly by the lab","It excludes remnant particles","It predicts risk better, at no cost","It has a single normal value"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It captures cholesterol in every atherogenic apoB (apolipoprotein B) lipoprotein, adds no cost, predicts atherosclerotic risk better than LDL-C, and is recommended for routine reporting. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What distinguishes a pseudoaneurysm at the puncture site from a simple hematoma?","choices":["It is larger but does not pulsate","It pulsates, and still communicates with the artery","It resolves without intervention in every case","It occurs only in the radial artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blood still moves in and out of it, which is why it can keep expanding and why the site is always checked. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which patients receive a pharmacological rather than an exercise stress test?","choices":["Those with a normal resting electrocardiogram","Those who cannot exercise","Those with renal impairment","Those under forty years of age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heart still has to be stressed; if the legs cannot do it, a drug does it instead. Usually because of a physical limitation. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which property allows an impulse to spread from one myocyte to the next?","choices":["Conductivity","Automaticity","Excitability","Contractility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is what turns a single cell's discharge into a coordinated wave across the whole muscle. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with sepsis has a prolonged prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time, low fibrinogen, a raised D-dimer and a low platelet count. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Immune thrombocytopenia","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Vitamin K antagonist effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the pattern of acute disseminated intravascular coagulation: prolonged screens, low fibrinogen and platelets, and a raised D-dimer. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a low suspicion of thrombosis, and her D-dimer is below the reference limit. What does the normal result do?","choices":["It confirms thrombosis","It shows the clotting factors are normal","It measures platelet function","It helps exclude thrombosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is best used for its high negative predictive value: a normal result helps exclude thrombosis. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has a prolonged prothrombin time with a low platelet count. Why can one abnormal value not diagnose disseminated intravascular coagulation?","choices":["The prothrombin time is always normal in it","Platelet counts rise in it","Only a bleeding time can diagnose it","Other conditions can cause each abnormality"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each abnormality has other causes, for example liver failure, vitamin K deficiency and major blood loss, so the overall picture matters. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A standard twelve-lead electrocardiogram uses how many physical electrodes?","choices":["Ten","Twelve","Six","Fifteen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each electrode is a different point of view, and the machine derives twelve views from the ten it is given. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has an isolated prolonged partial thromboplastin time that corrects on mixing. Which next test is indicated?","choices":["A Bethesda assay","A factor VIII assay","A dilute Russell viper venom time","A fibrinogen antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When a prolonged partial thromboplastin time corrects on mixing, evaluate for factor deficiency, beginning with the factor VIII assay. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does catheter ablation do during electrophysiological testing?","choices":["Stimulates the area to restore normal conduction","Places a permanent pacing wire in the atrium","Delivers a drug directly to the arrhythmic focus","Inactivates part of the heart by destroying that tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The tissue is not merely silenced but destroyed, which is what makes the treatment definitive. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A newborn has umbilical stump bleeding and lifelong severe bleeding later. The prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time and thrombin time are normal. Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor XIII","Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor IX"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Factor XIII deficiency gives umbilical stump bleeding and lifelong severe bleeding of any tissue with normal screening tests. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman had a borderline lipoprotein(a) before menopause. When is repeating it reasonable?","choices":["After starting a statin","After fasting overnight","After a viral illness","After menopause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Although a single lifetime measurement generally suffices, repeat measurement is reasonable in women after menopause, particularly if the premenopausal level was borderline. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which ion entering the cell during the plateau causes contraction?","choices":["Sodium","Potassium","Chloride","Calcium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the link between the electrical event and the mechanical one, which is why the plateau is where excitation becomes contraction. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 9"}]};
